-- results.mm0: the headline theorems of the development, restated in one place.
--
-- Nothing here is new: each theorem below is proved in one line from the theorem
-- named in its comment (results.auf). The file imports the whole development, so
-- checking it checks everything:
--
--   scripts/tg-check.sh spec/tg/results.mm0
--
-- Where things are:
--
--   tg.mm0       the axioms: Tarski–Grothendieck set theory with a choice operator,
--                as natural deduction. Everything else is definitions and theorems.
--   set/         the set-theory library (ordinals, least fixed points, universes,
--                arithmetic, trees, least models of Horn programs); `set/all.mm0`.
--   encoding/    Lean's syntax and the spec's syntactic relations, as trees and
--                Horn programs (generated from spec/lean.mm0 by scripts/tg-lean.py;
--                the 53 `enc_*` axioms say that the spec's terms are free).
--   model/       the model of the spec: the denotation of terms in an admissible
--                universe, the judgments as definitions, and every rule of the
--                spec as a theorem (`model/model-top.mm0`, `model/model-ci.mm0`).
--   lean-tg-rest.mm0
--                what a certificate is verified against in TG mode: the model and
--                whatever the spec states beyond it, which is nothing.
--   readback/    the model's pre-sets are the sets of the first universe, and
--                Lean sentences about them read back as sentences of set theory.

import "readback/model-fo.mm0";

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 1. Soundness and consistency of the spec
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Soundness is not one theorem: `deq`, `ty`, `inh` are definitions of the model
-- ("true in every admissible universe at every valuation"), and each of the
-- spec's rules is a theorem about them, under its own name (`beta`, `ind_iota_eq`,
-- `choice_ty`, ...), in model/model-top.mm0 and model/model-ci.mm0.

--| Consistency (`lean_consistent`, model/model-ci.mm0). No term has the type
--| `Π (p : Prop). p` in the empty context.
theorem consistency (e: expr): $ ⟮ nil ⊢ e : Π Prop . # 0 ⟯ $ > $ _ ⇒ ⊥ $;

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2. The model's pre-sets are the sets of the first universe
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- `b` is the atom of the block `pset b` (Lean's `PSet.{0}`), and `pset_rels b E M`
-- says that `E` and `M` are closed relations on it that provably satisfy the
-- characterizations of `PSet.Equiv` and `PSet.Mem`. `c ∊ fm ∅` is a sentence of the
-- language of set theory (a tree), `lsent b E M c` its Lean form, and `A ⊨ c` its
-- truth in `(A, ∈)`. `fv uni (suc ∅)` is the first universe, `U_1`.

--| Standardness (`pset_truth`, readback/model-fo.mm0). In every admissible universe
--| `u` and at every valuation `w` of the levels, the value of a sentence's Lean form
--| is inhabited exactly when the sentence is true in `(U_1, ∈)`.
theorem standardness {z: set} (G: hyps) (b: blk) (E M: expr) (u w c: set):
  $ pset_rels b E M $ > $ G ⇒ uadm u $ > $ G ⇒ w ∊ lvals $ > $ G ⇒ c ∊ fm ∅ $ >
  $ G ⇒ (∃ z (z ∊ dt u (mcv hfin) (lsent b E M c) w ∅) ↔ fv uni (suc ∅) ⊨ c) $;

--| Readback (`pset_readback`). A sentence of set theory whose Lean form is provable
--| is true in `(U_1, ∈)`.
theorem readback (b: blk) (E M T: expr) (c: set):
  $ pset_rels b E M $ > $ _ ⇒ c ∊ fm ∅ $ > $ enc_expr T ≐ lsent b E M c $ > $ inh nil T $ >
  $ _ ⇒ fv uni (suc ∅) ⊨ c $;

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3. The continuum hypothesis
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- `ch_code` is the continuum hypothesis as a sentence of set theory, and `r_ch U`
-- is the statement itself with its quantifiers bounded by `U`.

--| What the sentence means (`ch_sat`).
theorem ch_meaning (G: hyps) (U: class): $ G ⇒ isset U $ > $ G ⇒ (U ⊨ ch_code ↔ r_ch U) $;

--| If Lean proves the continuum hypothesis of its pre-sets, it holds in `U_1`
--| (`ch_readback`).
theorem ch_proved (b: blk) (E M T: expr):
  $ pset_rels b E M $ > $ enc_expr T ≐ lsent b E M ch_code $ > $ inh nil T $ >
  $ _ ⇒ r_ch (fv uni (suc ∅)) $;

--| If Lean refutes it, it fails in `U_1` (`nch_readback`).
theorem ch_refuted (b: blk) (E M T: expr):
  $ pset_rels b E M $ > $ enc_expr T ≐ lsent b E M (¬ₒ ch_code) $ > $ inh nil T $ >
  $ _ ⇒ ¬ r_ch (fv uni (suc ∅)) $;
